  data:  # 7de1
    kind: work_item  # 7de1
    title: "Enumerate the full I/O surface of rust's filesystem modules (std::fs, std::path, Path, PathBuf, os::windows::fs) so they can carry module_completeness"  # 7de1
    status: todo_hard  # 7de1
    priority: 2  # 7de1
    parent:  # 7de1
    tags:  # 7de1
    - io_boundary  # 7de1
    - verify_claims  # 7de1
    - rust  # 7de1
    - catalogue  # 7de1
    isbefore: []  # 7de1
    duplicate_of: []  # 7de1
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 7de1
    pr_ref:  # 7de1
    description: "WI-lutuh's rust audit (2026-08-26) mechanically probed all 33 stdlib modules the\ncorpus reaches and could grant `module_completeness: complete` to 16. This item\nis the filesystem share of the 17 it could not.\n\nMODULES: std::fs, std::path, std::path::Path, std::path::PathBuf,\nstd::os::windows::fs.\n\nWHY THEY REFUSE, and it is not a probe limitation: each genuinely exposes I/O.\n`std::path::Path` is the sharpest and is the exact analogue of the `pathlib`\nverdict the python audit's first draft got WRONG -- Path::exists / metadata /\nread_dir / canonicalize / symlink_metadata / read_link all touch the filesystem,\nso a Path is not a string type.\n\nWHAT `complete` ASSERTS, and why this is real work rather than a flag flip:\n`module_io_is_enumerated` means EVERY I/O primitive the module exposes is in the\ncatalogue. rust.yaml today carries a hand-picked slice -- std::fs has\n[read, read_to_string, read_dir, metadata, symlink_metadata, canonicalize] for\nfs_read and [write, create_dir, create_dir_all, remove_file, remove_dir,\nremove_dir_all, rename, copy, hard_link, soft_link] for fs_write. Whether that\nis the WHOLE surface is exactly what is unaudited. Off the top: read_link,\nset_permissions, symlink_metadata on DirEntry, DirBuilder, ReadDir's iterator\nitems, File's own trait impls. The audit is to enumerate the module's public\nsurface from rust-src and add every I/O-bearing name, not to confirm the\nexisting rows.\n\nEVIDENCE BAR, inherited from the python stdlib audit and NOT negotiable: total\nsurface (every surface the module exposes, not the ones this repo calls); EXACT\nmatching (std::fs does not vouch for std::fs::File); refuse-by-default, because\nthe wrong answer is a false all-clear. The python audit's first draft, written\nfrom expert knowledge unaided, got 5 of 32 verdicts wrong (16%) and EVERY error\nwas on the false-all-clear side. Use the probe, not recall.\n\nINSTRUMENT: ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/lutuh_rust_08262026/probe.py, which\nresolves a module or type to its rust-src definition and scans for syscall\nreach, I/O-trait delegation and I/O-bearing surface names. It REFUSES and never\ngrants; a candidate still needs adjudication.\n\nPAY EXPECTATION, stated so nobody is surprised: measured 2026-08-26, completing\nrust's stdlib audit changes NO verdict on either testable rust repo, because\n`boundary_coverage` is a ladder of early returns and the uncatalogued-module\ncheck is its LAST rung. See the note on WI-lutuh. This is necessary work whose\npayoff is gated behind rungs 1-4.\n\nTHE SAME DECOMPOSITION APPLIES PER LANGUAGE. WI-lutuh's scoping counted the\ndistinct stdlib modules each language's corpus reaches: go 53, python 53 still\nunenumerated after its 76-entry audit, erlang 38+, java 38+, elixir 32+, cpp 29,\njavascript 13. Only rust is decomposed here."  # 7de1
    fields: {}  # 7de1
- op: discuss  # 121e
  at: '2026-08-26T22:20:55Z'  # 121e
  by: agent  # 121e
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 121e
  clock: 2  # 121e
  nonce: 121e  # 121e
  message: "UNCATALOGUED PRIMITIVE FOUND IN THIS MODULE'S SURFACE, 2026-08-26. Noted here\nrather than filed separately, at the owner's direction, because it is squarely\nthis item's scope: it is part of std::fs's total surface, which is what this\nitem exists to enumerate.\n\nstd::fs::File EXPOSES FILE LOCKING AND rust.yaml HAS NO ROW FOR IT.\nMeasured against the installed stable toolchain\n(lib/rustlib/src/rust/library/std/src/fs.rs):\n\n  File::lock()             fs.rs:864\n  File::try_lock()         fs.rs:980\n  File::try_lock_shared()  fs.rs:1044\n  File::unlock()           fs.rs:1081\n\n`grep -n lock rust.yaml` returns NOTHING, so none of these can ever match the\ncatalogue. std::fs::File currently carries only [open], plus create/create_new\non the write side.\n\nWHY IT IS GENUINELY I/O and not a borderline call. Per its own doc comment this\nis `flock` with LOCK_EX on Unix and `LockFileEx` with LOCKFILE_EXCLUSIVE_LOCK on\nWindows. Another PROCESS calling flock on the same file blocks or fails. So it\nchanges state outside the calling program, observably, across a process\nboundary -- which is exactly the test the owner supplied while ruling WI-pavob,\nand it is the one case in that whole discussion that PASSES it. Thread\ncontention (std::sync::Mutex) failed the same test and was ruled out; file\nlocking is a different mechanism with a different answer.\n\nHOW IT WAS FOUND, worth recording because the probe did not surface it: it came\nout of a vocabulary discussion about std::sync, when the owner asked about \"file\ncontention\". The audit probe scans for syscall reach and I/O-trait delegation\nand would have flagged std::fs anyway -- but the SPECIFIC gap, that a\ncatalogued module's LOCKING surface is absent while its read/write surface is\npresent, is the kind of partial coverage this item's \"total surface, not the\nfunctions this repo calls\" bar exists to catch. A module can be present in the\ncatalogue and still be badly incomplete.\n\nOPEN SUB-QUESTION FOR WHOEVER DOES THIS ITEM, not decided here: which boundary\nkind does an advisory lock map to? Taking one does not change a single byte of\nthe file's contents, so a claim of \"never writes to the filesystem\" is not\nobviously violated -- yet it can block another program indefinitely, which is a\nreal effect on the outside world. fs_write is the closest existing kind and may\nsimply be right; it may also want its own row semantics. Decide it explicitly\nrather than by whichever kind happens to be convenient."  # 121e
- op: discuss  # dd9f
  at: '2026-08-26T23:10:54Z'  # dd9f
  by: agent  # dd9f
  actor: jgstern_agent  # dd9f
  clock: 3  # dd9f
  nonce: dd9f  # dd9f
  message: "AUDIT OF std::fs's TOTAL SURFACE, 2026-08-26, against the installed stable\ntoolchain (library/std/src/fs.rs). This is the \"total surface, not the functions\nthis repo calls\" bar this item sets, applied.\n\nMETHOD, and the first attempt was WRONG so it is recorded. A regex that tracked\nthe enclosing `impl` block mis-attributed every FREE function appearing after an\nimpl to that impl -- sixteen module-level functions were reported as members of\n`impl DirEntry as AsInner`. Caught by disbelieving an implausible intermediate\n(DirEntry does not have a `create_dir_all` method), not by any error. Re-derived\nusing INDENTATION as the discriminator: column-0 `pub fn` is free, indented is a\nmethod. EVERY probe bug in this campaign has produced a plausible-looking wrong\nanswer.\n\nSTABILITY WAS CHECKED, because cataloguing a nightly-only API is its own error.\nExcluded as UNSTABLE: fs::set_times, fs::set_times_nofollow,\nfs::set_permissions_nofollow, File::open_buffered, File::create_buffered,\nDir::open, Dir::open_file.\n\nWHAT IS MISSING. rust.yaml catalogues 19 std::fs entries. The stable I/O surface\nis roughly 37. So the module is catalogued at about HALF, and every gap below is\ngenuine I/O:\n\n  FREE FUNCTIONS -- 16 of 19 stable ones catalogued. Missing:\n    exists           fs_read    stats the path\n    read_link        fs_read    reads a symlink target\n    set_permissions  fs_write   mutates file metadata\n\n  std::fs::File -- 3 of ~15 catalogued, and all three are ASSOCIATED FUNCTIONS\n  (open / create / create_new). NOT ONE METHOD IS CATALOGUED. Missing, all\n  stable:\n    metadata          fs_read    fstat\n    set_len           fs_write   truncates\n    set_permissions   fs_write   fchmod\n    set_times         fs_write   futimens\n    sync_all          fs_write   fsync\n    sync_data         fs_write   fdatasync\n    lock, lock_shared, try_lock, try_lock_shared, unlock\n                                 flock / LockFileEx -- the cross-process\n                                 locking already noted separately on this item\n\n  std::fs::DirBuilder -- NOT CATALOGUED AT ALL.\n    create           fs_write   mkdir (recursive() is a builder setter, no I/O)\n\n  std::fs::DirEntry -- NOT CATALOGUED AT ALL.\n    metadata         fs_read    stat\n    file_type        fs_read?   may stat on some platforms -- JUDGMENT CALL\n\nCORRECTLY ABSENT, recorded so a later auditor does not \"fix\" them. These are\nin-memory snapshots and setters that perform NO syscall: Metadata (accessed,\ncreated, modified, len, permissions, is_dir/is_file/is_symlink), Permissions\n(readonly, set_readonly), FileTimes (new, set_accessed, set_modified), FileType\n(is_dir, is_file, is_symlink), OpenOptions builder setters (append, create,\ncreate_new, read, truncate, write -- only `open` does I/O, and it is already\ncatalogued), DirEntry (path, file_name), File::options.\n\nTWO JUDGMENT CALLS NOT DECIDED HERE:\n  * File::try_clone -- dup(2). A syscall, but no data crosses and nothing\n    outside the process changes. Under the owner's WI-pavob test (\"is it\n    changing some state outside of itself\") this is OUT, same as a mutex.\n    Proposed OUT; flagging rather than assuming.\n  * DirEntry::file_type -- returns a cached type on platforms whose readdir\n    supplies d_type, and stats otherwise. Platform-dependent I/O. Proposed IN\n    as fs_read on refuse-by-default grounds.\n\nTHE HEADLINE FOR WI-lutuh: this module was ALREADY IN THE CATALOGUE and\ntherefore never appeared on any \"unexamined module\" list, while half its I/O\nsurface was absent. Catalogue PRESENCE is not catalogue COVERAGE, and only a\ntotal-surface audit can tell them apart."  # dd9f
- op: discuss  # 4448
  at: '2026-08-27T01:17:06Z'  # 4448
  by: agent  # 4448
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 4448
  clock: 4  # 4448
  nonce: '4448'  # 4448
  message: "std::fs / std::path ROWS WRITTEN, 2026-08-27. This closes the FIRST of this\nitem's five modules by enumeration, and settles the three decisions it flagged.\n\n20 STABLE I/O PRIMITIVES ADDED across six modules:\n  std::fs             exists, read_link, set_permissions\n  std::fs::File       metadata, set_len, set_permissions, set_times, sync_all,\n                      sync_data, lock, lock_shared, try_lock, try_lock_shared,\n                      unlock                              (ELEVEN METHODS, NONE\n                                                           previously present)\n  std::fs::DirBuilder create\n  std::fs::DirEntry   metadata, file_type\n  std::path::Path     try_exists, is_symlink\n  std::path           absolute\n\nTHE THREE OPEN DECISIONS, all settled, all reversible:\n  * File::try_clone -> OUT. dup(2) is a syscall but nothing crosses and nothing\n    outside the process changes; that is the owner's WI-pavob test, and it is\n    the same verdict the mutex got.\n  * DirEntry::file_type -> IN as fs_read. Cached where readdir supplies d_type,\n    stats otherwise; included on refuse-by-default grounds.\n  * THE lock FAMILY -> fs_write, and the item asked for this to be decided\n    explicitly rather than conveniently. It changes no byte, so fs_write\n    OVERSTATES it -- but a lock is observable to another PROCESS, which is the\n    boundary test std::sync::Mutex failed and the one case in that whole\n    discussion that PASSES. Omitting it is a false all-clear. fs_write is the\n    only one of the nine kinds that carries it without a new HEAVYWEIGHT axis\n    (ADR-0023/0027/0028 registry + ADR-0024 scaffolding) and the residual\n    over-claim is narrow: it can only mislead a program that opens READ-ONLY\n    and locks. Recorded in the catalogue notes as reversible -- a dedicated\n    kind is the alternative if it proves noisy.\n  std::path::absolute is fs_read by PRECEDENT, not by taste: it delegates to\n  env::current_dir() (getcwd(2)), and std::env::current_dir is already fs_read.\n\nTWO PROBE BUGS, both returning a PLAUSIBLE WRONG ANSWER rather than an error,\nwhich is now three for three in this campaign:\n  * the impl-tracking regex from the earlier audit (16 free functions\n    attributed to impl DirEntry) -- re-derived using INDENTATION.\n  * a signature regex requiring \"(\" immediately after the name, so EVERY\n    generic `pub fn exists<P: AsRef<Path>>(..)` was silently skipped. That is\n    why DirBuilder::create first appeared ABSENT FROM THE TOOLCHAIN. Widening\n    it also CORRECTED THE FILED KIND: create takes &self and is a METHOD, not\n    the associated function this item had recorded.\nA PROBE THAT UNDER-REPORTS LOOKS EXACTLY LIKE A MODULE THAT HAS NO SURFACE.\n\nPath::is_absolute was an I/O-reach FALSE POSITIVE -- sys::path::is_absolute is\npath.has_root(), purely lexical. Caught by disbelieving it, not by an error.\nThe refusals are now PINNED BY TESTS so a later auditor cannot \"fix\" them.\n\nMEASURED BEFORE SHIPPING, because on this predicate a CORRECT row is exactly\nwhat used to regress a verdict. 42 reusable surveys, both arms driving the\nPRODUCTION loader and the PRODUCTION predicate (old arm = HEAD's rust.yaml\nbytes): starved-module set UNCHANGED in every repo -- nothing freed, nothing\nnewly starved. THE INSTRUMENT IS SHOWN LIVE, not assumed: 9 of 42 surveys carry\na non-empty starved set, and std::path::Path starves in exactly 3 of 3 rust\nrepos, reproducing the independently recorded figure. std::fs::File becomes\nMIXED-kind here and it is INV-soval that keeps File::open satisfying it; a test\nfails if that fix is reverted underneath these rows.\n\nSTILL OPEN ON THIS ITEM -- IT IS NOT DONE:\n  * std::path::PathBuf, std::os::windows::fs NOT YET AUDITED.\n  * PathBuf DEREFS TO Path, so `pb.exists()` may be attributed to PathBuf. NOT\n    resolved by adding PathBuf method rows: that would make PathBuf method-only,\n    and any repo calling PathBuf::from (which performs no I/O and is correctly\n    uncatalogued) would then STARVE. Deciding this needs the same measurement\n    run, not a guess.\n  * module_completeness grants are the NEXT arc step, not this one. These rows\n    are the enumeration that makes a grant possible; no grant is claimed here.\n\nTHE CATALOGUE HEADER ASSERTED A COMPLETENESS THIS AUDIT FALSIFIED, and that is\ncorrected in the same commit. WI-tukif wrote \"coverage is complete for the\nstrict-stdlib IO surface\" in May, from a doc index, NAMING std::fs and\nstd::path::Path among the modules it covered. Twenty stable primitives were\nmissing from exactly those modules. The header now withdraws the claim and\nnames what IS audited (by enumeration, rustc 1.94.0) and what is NOT\n(std::io, std::net, std::process, std::env, std::path::PathBuf), so the next\nreader is not misled the same way. A COMPLETENESS CLAIM WRITTEN FROM A DOC\nINDEX IS NOT THE SAME EVIDENCE AS AN ENUMERATION OF THE SURFACE.\n\nstd::os::windows::fs IS AN OPEN CONFLICT AND IS DELIBERATELY NOT IN THIS PR.\nThis item names it in scope. The catalogue header excludes \"std::os::* raw-fd\nextensions\" (WI-tukif / ADR-0016) -- and the noun there is the raw-fd extension\ntraits, which are meta-IO and correctly out. But symlink_file / symlink_dir are\nplain filesystem mutation, not raw-fd anything, and the cross-platform\nequivalent std::fs::soft_link IS catalogued. So the exclusion reads as an\ninconsistency rather than a decision, and overriding another item's recorded\nscope silently is not the way to settle it. Recorded in the header; needs a\nruling. Stable surface at issue: symlink_file, symlink_dir, FileExt::seek_read\n(fs_read), FileExt::seek_write (fs_write). junction_point and seek_read_buf are\nUNSTABLE and out regardless. OpenOptionsExt / MetadataExt / FileTypeExt /\nFileTimesExt are builder setters and in-memory accessors.\n\nA TEST-SELECTION GAP WAS FOUND SHIPPING THIS AND IS FILED AS INV-bigaz. The\nslicer walks Python imports, so a YAML catalogue has no edge to its loader:\nthis change reported \"changed source files: 0\" and selected ONE test file, for\na data file 19 test files (1,648 tests) read in 27 seconds. CI runs the\nmanifest, and its force-full-suite escape names seven .py files and no data\ndirectory. Worked around by manually extending the manifest, with a comment.\nA MANUAL EXTENSION IS NOT A FIX -- it depended on my noticing that \"1 test\nfile\" was implausible for the change I had just made."  # 4448
